    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Germans achieved total surprise on 16 December 1944 through Allied overconfidence, faulty intelligence, poor aerial reconnaissance in bad weather and a preoccupation with Allied offensive plans elsewhere.</li><li>American resistance around Elsenborn Ridge in the north and Bastogne in the south blocked key roads, and the congestion and defender-friendly terrain put the German advance behind schedule.</li><li>The battle severely depleted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s armoured forces, which remained largely unreplaced for the rest of the war, and it was the last major offensive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> attempted on the Western Front.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When German infiltrators in American uniforms caused alarm behind the Allied lines, military policemen quizzed troops on things every American was expected to know, and General Omar Bradley was briefly detained after he correctly named Springfield as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, because the policeman believed it was Chicago.</p></aside>
    </section>

