      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">September 1944</span><span class="rf-text">Hitler first outlines a counter-offensive to his generals on 16 September</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1944</span><span class="rf-text">On 16 December the Germans open the offensive with a 90-minute barrage and achieve total surprise</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1944</span><span class="rf-text">On 17 December prisoners are shot near Baugnez in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a> massacre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1944</span><span class="rf-text">On 19 December the 101st Airborne Division reaches Bastogne by truck, and the town is cut off on 20 December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1944</span><span class="rf-text">On 24 December the furthest German advance is stopped at Foy-Notre-Dame by the U.S. 2nd Armored Division</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1944</span><span class="rf-text">On 26 December the lead element of Patton's Third Army reaches Bastogne from the south and ends the siege</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1945</span><span class="rf-text">On 16 January the U.S. First and Third Armies link up at Houffalize</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">January 1945</span><span class="rf-text">The offensive closes on 25 January, though the last German units do not return to their start line until February</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-bulge" data-v="149d281fa9" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Three German armies face the Ardennes front</b> The Sixth Panzer Army stood in the north, the Fifth Panzer Army in the middle and the Seventh Army in the south. The U.S. 99th and 2nd Divisions, the 106th Division and VIII Corps held the line opposite them.</li><li><b>The assault opens and Peiper's group leads the north</b> The Germans opened with a 90-minute artillery barrage and attacked all along the front. Kampfgruppe Peiper, charged with the main effort, left the Sixth Panzer Army's line late.</li><li><b>Two regiments of the 106th Division are surrounded</b> The Fifth Panzer Army surrounded two largely intact regiments of the 106th Division in a pincer movement and forced their surrender.</li><li><b>The northern shoulder holds and Peiper turns south</b> The 99th and 2nd Divisions kept the Sixth Panzer Army from the ridge and the roads west of it. Peiper turned south round Hünningen and reached Stavelot, where the Americans delayed him.</li><li><b>St. Vith slows the Fifth Panzer Army</b> The defenders of St. Vith, led by the 7th Armored Division, resisted the German attacks and slowed the advance. The 2nd Panzer Division spearheaded the Fifth Panzer Army.</li><li><b>Bastogne is surrounded</b> The 101st Airborne Division reached Bastogne by truck on 19 December. The panzer columns swung past on either side and the Germans had surrounded the town by 21 December.</li><li><b>The advance stops short of the Meuse; Bastogne is relieved</b> Parts of the 2nd Panzer Division came in sight of the Meuse at Foy-Notre-Dame on 24 December, and the U.S. 2nd Armored Division attacked them there. On 26 December the 4th Armored Division broke through to Bastogne.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower.jpg" alt="Dwight D. Eisenhower" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dwight_D._Eisenhower,_official_photo_portrait,_May_29,_1959_(cropped)(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">White House, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the breakout from Normandy at the end of July 1944 and the Allied landings in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 15 August 1944, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> advanced towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> more quickly than anticipated. Troops were fatigued, supply lines were stretched extremely thin and supplies were dangerously depleted. The Allies captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> intact in the first days of September, but it was not operational until 28 November, because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Schelde</a> estuary had to be cleared of German troops and naval mines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> and Field Marshal Montgomery differed over whether Montgomery or Lieutenant General Omar Bradley would get priority access to supplies, and Eisenhower preferred a broad-front strategy.</p><p>The German situation remained dire. There were 96 Allied divisions at or near the front, and the Germans could field a total of 55 understrength divisions. Hitler first outlined his counter-offensive to his generals on 16 September 1944. He believed that a blow in the west would split the Allied forces and compel the Americans and British to settle for a separate peace. After the war this assessment was generally viewed as unrealistic, given Allied air superiority.</p><p>By December Eisenhower and his staff had decided to hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> primarily as a rest area for the U.S. First Army. The line there was thinly held, because of the favourable defensive terrain and because the Allies had intelligence that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> was using the area across the German border as a rest-and-refit area.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Bulge&#8221; cites where it covers Allied supply issues, Drafting the offensive, Operation names, Planning:</p><ul><li>Samuel W. Mitcham, Panzers in Winter: Hitler's Army and the Battle of the Bulge (Praeger, 2006)</li><li>John Toland, Battle: The Story of the Bulge (University of Nebraska Press, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Germans committed over 410,000 men, just over 1,400 tanks and armoured fighting vehicles, 2,600 artillery pieces and over 1,000 combat aircraft. Allied forces eventually numbered more than 700,000 men. Field Marshal Walter Model's Army Group B and Field Marshal Gerd von Rundstedt, commanding OB West, were put in charge of the operation. The 6th Panzer Army under Sepp Dietrich had the lead role and the shortest route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>; the Fifth Panzer Army under Hasso von Manteuffel was to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>; and the Seventh Army under Erich Brandenberger, with four infantry divisions, was to protect the southern flank.</p><p>The plan originally called for just under 45 divisions, but a manpower shortage reduced the force to around 30. Among them were Volksgrenadier units formed from veterans and from recruits formerly regarded as too young, too old or too frail to fight. Four conditions were thought critical: complete surprise, poor weather to neutralise Allied air power, a rapid advance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> by day four, and the capture of Allied fuel, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> estimated that its fuel covered only one third to one half of the ground to Antwerp. Model and von Rundstedt thought Antwerp too ambitious and proposed a smaller plan, but Hitler would not listen. Operation Greif, in which English-speaking Germans in American and British uniforms were to disrupt the rear, was added.</p><p>The U.S. units in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> were a mixture of inexperienced troops, such as the 99th and 106th Divisions, and battle-hardened troops sent to recuperate, such as the 28th Infantry Division.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Bulge&#8221; cites where it covers German plans, German forces held up:</p><ul><li>Steven Zaloga, Battle of the Bulge 1944 (Osprey, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At 05:30 on 16 December 1944 the Germans began a 90-minute barrage by 1,600 artillery pieces across a front of 130 kilometres. Heavy snowstorms kept Allied aircraft grounded but also hampered the German advance, and poor traffic control led to traffic jams and fuel shortages in forward units. In the north, the U.S. 2nd and 99th Infantry Divisions held Elsenborn Ridge and forced the German spearhead onto less favourable routes further south. Kampfgruppe Peiper, of 4,800 men and 600 vehicles, was more than 16 hours behind schedule; on 17 December SS troops shot prisoners near Baugnez, and at least 84 American prisoners were murdered. Peiper reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stavelot">Stavelot</a> and La Gleize but found bridges blown and fuel short.</p><p>In the centre, the Fifth Panzer Army surrounded two regiments of the 106th Division on the Schnee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> and forced their surrender. The defenders of St. Vith, led by the 7th Armored Division, slowed the advance until the town was evacuated on 21 December. In the south, the 101st Airborne Division reached Bastogne by truck on 19 December, and the Germans surrounded the town on 20 December. When the German commander asked for its surrender, Brigadier General Anthony McAuliffe replied "NUTS!".</p><p>The weather began to clear on 23 December, allowing Allied air attacks. On 24 December the furthest German advance, at Foy-Notre-Dame, was stopped. On 26 December Company D, 37th Tank Battalion of the 4th Armored Division reached Bastogne at 16:50, ending the siege.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Bulge&#8221; cites where it covers Initial German assault, Units involved in initial assault, Attack on the northern shoulder, Best German divisions assigned:</p><ul><li>Maj. Han Bouwmeester, Beginning of the End: The Leadership of SS Obersturmbannführer Jochen Peiper (Royal Netherlands Army, Free University of Amsterdam, the Netherlands, 2004)</li><li>Bruce Quarrie, The Ardennes Offensive VI Panzer Armee (Osprey, 1999)</li><li>John Toland, Battle: The Story of the Bulge (University of Nebraska Press, 1999)</li><li>Steven Zaloga, Battle of the Bulge 1944 (Osprey, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Montgomery launched his attack from the north on 3 January 1945, two days after the date <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> wanted, and Patton's Third Army fought from the south. On 7 and 8 January Hitler agreed to withdraw gradually from the tip of the salient. The First and Third Armies linked up at Houffalize on 16 January, the Americans recaptured St. Vith on 23 January, and the last German units did not return to their start line until February. On 1 January the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> attacked Allied airfields in Operation Bodenplatte, destroying or severely damaging some 465 aircraft and losing 277 planes, and Operation Nordwind began against the U.S. Seventh Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, closing on 25 January.</p><p>Counts of the casualties differ. German losses are put at between 63,000 and 104,000 killed, missing, wounded or captured, and Allied casualties at 77,000 to more than 83,000, including at least 8,600 killed. American soldiers killed about sixty German prisoners near Chenogne on 1 January, and eleven black American soldiers were tortured and shot at Wereth on 17 December; the perpetrators were never punished.</p><p>The German offensive inflicted heavy losses and set back the Allied invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> by several weeks, but the Germans' last reserves were gone and the Luftwaffe was shattered. On 12 January the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>–Oder offensive, brought forward from 20 January.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Bulge&#8221; cites where it covers Outcome, Allied, German:</p><ul><li>Peter Caddick-Adams, Snow and Steel: The Battle of the Bulge, 1944–45 (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ambrose"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ambrose.jpg" alt="Ambrose" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmbroseOfMilan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of the Allied side of the story rests on intelligence records. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bletchley-park">Bletchley Park</a>, F. L. Lucas and Peter Calvocoressi of Hut 3 wrote a report on the handling of Ultra before the battle, concluding that the intelligence was plentiful but misread and misused. A report by the Chief of the Secret Intelligence Service, issued on 28 December 1944, drew similar lessons and stressed over-reliance on Ultra and poor Allied security. The U.S. Army's official history is the source for the loss of at least 7,000 men on the Schnee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a>, probably 8,000 or 9,000.</p><p>Several accounts are disputed or come second hand. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a> killing vary, and Peiper, Dietrich and others were tried for it. Dietrich's postwar story about his plan to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> shifted under questioning. Manteuffel's praise of Montgomery comes from interviews by B. H. Liddell Hart, who has been accused of putting words in the mouths of German generals. Casualty totals vary with the source: the German High Command accepted 81,834 for its own losses, while the U.S. Army Center of Military History gives 100,000 German and 75,000 American casualties.</p><p>Historians also disagree over weight. John S. D. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> called the action of the 2nd and 99th Divisions on the northern shoulder possibly the most decisive of the campaign. Stephen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> wrote in 1997 that putting Montgomery in command in the north had no effect on the battle, while John Buckley wrote in 2013 that Montgomery must take considerable credit for stabilising it. There were no correspondents around St. Vith, Elsenborn or Monschau-Höfen.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="Winston Churchill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Winston_Churchill_-_19086236948_(restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Battle of the Bulge was the largest and bloodiest single battle fought by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and it marked the last major offensive attempted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> on the Western Front. Its names reflect different views: the Germans used Unternehmen Wacht am Rhein, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> called it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> Counteroffensive, and the press coined "Bulge" from the shape of the front on news maps. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> told the House of Commons that it was undoubtedly the greatest American battle of the war and would, he believed, be regarded as an ever-famous American victory.</p><p>The battle left a quarrel behind. At a press conference on 7 January, Montgomery gave the impression, in the ears of American leaders, that he had taken the lion's share of the credit. Bradley and Patton threatened to resign unless his command was changed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Eisenhower</a> decided to dismiss him before the chiefs of staff, de Guingand and Walter Smith, moved him to reconsider. Montgomery later wrote that he should never have held the conference.</p><p>Because of troop shortages, Eisenhower decided to integrate the service for the first time during the battle, with more than 2,000 black soldiers volunteering to go to the front, an important step towards a desegregated U.S. military. After the war the U.S. Army issued the Ardennes-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> campaign citation to units that took part. Bastogne, with its resupply by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">parachute</a> and glider and Patton's fast response, captured the public's imagination in the press.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/die-wacht-am-rhein">Die Wacht am Rhein<span>1840</span></a><a href="/songs/white-christmas">White Christmas<span>c. 1942</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ardennes Offensive began on 16 December 1944 with a surprise German attack on thinly held American lines. It was halted short of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> by the end of December, and the front was restored by the end of January 1945. Casualties on each side ran to tens of thousands.</em></p>

